<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="BOOOST Performance Test Suite">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Covers the boost-auth-api happy path end to end: /v1/auth/otp -&gt; /v1/auth/verify -&gt; /v1/identity/register -&gt; /v1/identity/status -&gt; /v1/identity/profile -&gt; /v1/auth/logout. Built against the Mobile Integration Guide for boost-auth-api (dev=api-dev.booost.com, ppe=api-ppe.booost.com, prd=api.booost.com -- tokens are environment-scoped, a dev token will not validate in ppe). Test identities generated with DOB 2000-01-01 so dev/ppe skip SMS and accept any 6-digit OTP (TM-prefixed member ids, confirmed never happens in production).

RATE LIMIT WARNING, read before sizing any run: /v1/auth/otp is limited to 3 requests per phone per hour and 10 per IP per hour by default (429 rate_limit_exceeded). A thread pool that recycles a small CSV across thousands of iterations will mostly measure the rate limiter, not auth performance -- size the identity pool to at least (planned login attempts within any rolling hour) / 3. For the 4,000-5,000 user range in the Oct 2026 PPE performance brief, generate and pre-register a pool of at least 5,000 distinct identities before running the Login/Authentication group at that scale. Also confirm with the backend team whether PPE's per-IP limit will be raised for the test window -- several JMeter load generators sharing few egress IPs can trip the 10/hour per-IP limit even with a large phone pool.

SA ID ENTROPY CEILING -- CONFIRMED LIVE, BLOCKS THE 5,000-IDENTITY TARGET AS-IS (2026-10-21): the fixed DOB=2000-01-01 SMS-bypass trick only leaves a 4-digit sequence number free in the SA ID (yymmdd is forced to 000101, citizen/gender digits are policy-fixed) -- about 10,000 valid distinct IDs EVER, for ALL teams/time that have used this same convention against this PPE environment, not just this run. Reproduced directly against api-ppe.booost.com: a freshly-generated identity on this DOB got 409 identity_in_use on /verify ("This identity document is already registered to a different phone number") against an ID that had never been used in this session -- i.e. it collided with someone else's prior test registration. Birthday-paradox math says 5,000 draws from a ~10,000-slot space produces collisions as the norm, not the exception. This is a test-tooling ceiling, not an application performance finding -- a progressive run hitting rising 409/403 rates at higher user counts would be this artifact, not BOOOST degrading. Needs resolving with the backend/MIS team before trusting the 5,000-user registration numbers: either a wider/dedicated bypass range, a pre-allocated ID block for this test window, or accepting retry-on-collision logic added to this script (not yet done).

GRAPHQL GATEWAY FOUND AND CONFIRMED LIVE (2026-10-21): after api-ppe.booost.com/graphql (plain 404, no route at all) and apic-ppe.booost.com (uniform Cloudflare block on every path, not probed further since that's a security control) both proved to be dead ends, the real gateway is host=app-ppe.booost.com, path=/v2/ -- confirmed independently with a fresh access_token from the proven login flow: POST https://app-ppe.booost.com/v2/ with a DashboardPage query returned 200 with real data (real voucher/deal/history rows from the PPE environment). graphqlHost and graphqlPath now default to these confirmed values (still -J overridable if the gateway ever moves). The real DashboardPage query (header, unclaimedBadges, tapTapBoosts, vouchers, deals, history, historyDateRangeFilter, legal) is now wired into the Home Dashboard sampler, verified end to end through this exact JMX file (03_HomeDashboard -> 200, see README).

ALL TEN SCENARIOS NOW CONFIRMED REAL VIA SCHEMA INTROSPECTION (2026-10-21): with the gateway found, ran a GraphQL introspection query (query { __schema { queryType { fields { name } } } }) against it -- allowed, returned the entire query schema in one call. That revealed the real field for every remaining scenario, then each was individually confirmed live (200, real PPE data) through this exact file: DashboardPage (Home), BoostBuyPage + VouchersPage + VoucherOffering(id) (Vouchers -- landing, catalogue, and open-detail, a 3-step chain, not the single guessed query this used to be), FutureBoostPage + FutureBoostDetailPage(offeringId) (Future Boost -- load + open-detail, offeringId extracted dynamically from the first query's response via JSONPostProcessor, not hardcoded), BalancePage(currency) (Balance/History -- one call covers both, including transactionHistory as a sub-field; takes a required currency arg), DealsPage (Deals), BoostAMillionPage (BAM), and PartnerList + PartnerPage(partnerId) (Partner/Loyalty -- list + open-partner, partnerId also extracted dynamically). The detail-page calls prove the "open detail"/"open partner" steps the brief's journeys ask for, not just the landing load. Wired into Progressive Load (the full sequential journey), Mixed Journey, and Soak Endurance alike -- Mixed Journey ran all 9 branches in one pass, 200 across every one, 0 errors.

NOT YET DONE: the Deals scenario's own "open detail" step (a `deal(id: ID!)` query exists and was found via introspection, but every deals list returned empty during testing, so there was no real ID to verify it against -- left unwired rather than guessing). The Profile *write* (PATCH) stays unbuilt per the brief's own instruction to keep that read-based. Weighting Mixed Journey's 9 real branches by the brief's percentages (20/20/20/10/10/5/5/5/5): JMeter's ThroughputController in "Percent Executions" mode per branch, still not done -- now that every branch is real, this is the next thing actually worth doing.

HOW TO RUN EACH SCENARIO -- one parameterized plan, scenario selection is via thread-count properties (0 = that group does nothing), not by hand-editing the file:

  Registration Load (new, never-registered identities):
    jmeter -n -t this.jmx -JtotalUsers=N -Jcsvfile=pool.csv -JregUsers=N -JregRampup=secs -JregLoops=1
    (totalUsers drives the Username Generator; it must be set explicitly and match
    regUsers -- it defaults to 0/generate-nothing specifically so an unrelated run
    targeting a different group can never silently overwrite an existing pool.)

  Login / Authentication (needs an ALREADY-REGISTERED pool -- run Registration Load once first against the
  same -Jcsvfile to create it, then point Login at that same file):
    jmeter -n -t this.jmx -Jcsvfile=pool.csv -JloginUsers=N -JloginRampup=secs -JloginLoops=cycles

  Test 1 (baseline): small N (e.g. 10) on both groups above, separately.
  Test 2 (progressive): re-run Login with -JloginUsers stepped 250/500/1000/2000/3000/4000/5000, same pool
    reused each step (CSVDataSet recycles).
  Test 4 (login spike): short -JloginRampup (concentrated) at 500/1000/2000.
  Test 5 (registration load): step -JregUsers 100/250/500/1000 (fresh pool/generated count each step).
  Test 6 (spike)/7 (stress)/8 (soak): same Login group, vary -JloginUsers / -JloginLoops / -JloginRampup --
    no separate JMX needed.

  Payments Order Flow is OUT OF SCOPE for this round per the Oct 2026 brief -- left in the plan (num_threads
  defaults to 0, opt in explicitly with -JpayUsers=N if ever needed again).

Scenarios 3-9 (Home, Balance/History, Future Boost, Vouchers, Deals, BAM,
Partner/Loyalty) are now built as real GraphQL samplers inside "2 - Login
Authentication" (toggle the whole loop with -JincludeBrowsing), per the
Oct 2026 BFF/GraphQL scope doc -- see the GRAPHQL GATEWAY finding above
before trusting them, the endpoint isn't reachable at the specified host
yet. The Profile *write* (PATCH /v1/identity/profile) is documented too
but the brief says keep profile/settings read-based for this round, so
it's still not built. "4 - Mixed Journey" still has these same 7 journeys
as disabled placeholders with TODO paths (not yet replaced with the real
GraphQL calls built into group 2) -- see performance/README.md for what's
left to wire there.</stringProp>
